{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:InformationTheory.IncompletenessImpliesHorizon","@type":"LeanModule","name":"InformationTheory.IncompletenessImpliesHorizon","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Nat.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Logic.Function.Basic"],"declarations":12,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.computable_fragment_proper","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.computable_fragment_proper","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L122-L130","doc":"The computable fragment is strictly smaller than the full\nfunction space: there exist functions in (ℕ → ℕ) that are\nnot in the image of eval. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.noncomputableWitness_spec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.noncomputableWitness_spec","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L108-L113","doc":"The witness is indeed non-computable. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.noncomputableWitness","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.noncomputableWitness","kind":"def","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L101-L106","doc":"The non-computable function is explicitly constructible:\nit is the diagonal of the evaluation table.  This is not\na mere existence proof — the witness is computable relative\nto an oracle for the enumeration. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.exists_noncomputable","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.exists_noncomputable","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L79-L99","doc":"**Main theorem: incompleteness implies non-computable functions.**\n\nIn any Turing-complete resolution system with ℕ-embedding\n(i.e., programs are countable and compute functions on ℕ),\nthere exist functions not computable by any program.\n\nThis is the formal content of Gödel's first incompleteness\ntheorem applied to the computational framework: the computable\nfragment Γ_fin(S, A₀) is strictly smaller than the full\nfunction space.\n\nThe non-computable function populates the computational horizon\nH_c = V(h_c): it is a degree-0 section that exists in the full\nalgebra but cannot be produced by any finite braid. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:incompleteness-implies-horizon","label":"prop:incompleteness-implies-horizon","md":"quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.diagonal_not_in_range","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.diagonal_not_in_range","kind":"theorem","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L70-L77","doc":"The diagonal function differs from every enumerated function.\nFor each n: d(n) = enum(n)(n) + 1 ≠ enum(n)(n), so d ≠ enum(n). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.diagonal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.diagonal","kind":"def","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L60-L68","doc":"The diagonal function: given an enumeration of functions,\nconstruct a function that differs from each enumerated function\nat the self-application point.\n\nd(n) = enum(n)(n) + 1\n\nThis is the Gödel–Turing diagonal construction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem.eval","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem.eval","kind":"def","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L56-L56","doc":"Each program computes a total function ℕ → ℕ. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem.enumerate_surj","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem.enumerate_surj","kind":"theorem","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L54-L54","doc":"The enumeration is surjective (every program has an index). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem.enumerate","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem.enumerate","kind":"def","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L52-L52","doc":"Programs are countable: ℕ surjects onto P. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem.Program","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem.Program","kind":"def","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L50-L50","doc":"The type of programs (finite braids). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem.mk","kind":"ctor","line":48,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L48-L48","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.IncompletenessHorizon.TuringCompleteSystem","kind":"structure","line":41,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/information-theory/incompleteness-implies-horizon.lean#L41-L56","doc":"A Turing-complete resolution system: programs are countable\n(surjectively enumerable from ℕ) and each computes a total\nfunction ℕ → ℕ.\n\nThe ℕ domain of `eval` is the arithmetic non-triviality\ncondition: the system can represent natural numbers.  A trivial\ncategory (which cannot encode ℕ) does not admit such a system. ","signatureChunk":0}]}